From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:36:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10406/11362] Root-cause the recurring codex/gemini model
 mismatch

`Codex CLI error: Model metadata for 'gemini-3.1-pro-preview' not found` has
fired 15 times across 5 dates since 2026-07-01, always exactly 3 per date -- one
task burning its whole retry budget and retiring for an environmental reason.

The cause is a half-applied coercion in research_conductor.py:

  6579  task_model = task.get("model")     # read BEFORE the coercion
  6627  task_agent_type = "codex"          # agent flipped
  6695  model_override=task_model,         # model NOT flipped

The gemini->codex coercion rewrites agent_type in four places and never touches
task_model, so codex is invoked with a Gemini model name.

The interesting part is that the safety net WORKED and still produced this. The
coercion exists precisely because the planner keeps emitting gemini routing that
Codex-Default-v2 retired in June -- and it fired correctly, then handed the new
agent the old agent's model. A half-applied coercion is worse than none: without
it the task fails as gemini and is legible; with it, it fails as CODEX with an
error that reads like a codex problem. That is why five occurrences went untraced.

Base rate counted from the log rather than estimated: intervals +4, +31, +22, +3
days. Irregular, with the two most recent gaps the shortest. An earlier claim of
"roughly monthly" in chat was wrong twice -- the deltas were eyeballed instead of
computed, and a fifth date was missed by reading truncated output as complete.
Both errors are recorded in the entry rather than quietly dropped, because the
base-rate check only helps if the arithmetic is actually done.

The fix and its test invariant are specified: a coerced task must never keep a
model belonging to the agent it was coerced away from. Not implemented here --
this commit is the diagnosis.

